WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.490
Film Courage:&nbsp; There are critics who argue
that popular culture is “dumbing down.”&nbsp;

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:11.540
Would you say this is true, reflected in the
film and television of today?

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.920
Karl Iglesias:&nbsp; I think I’m on the fence
about that.&nbsp;

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:21.150
It really depends on what movies and TV are
very popular.&nbsp;

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:32.980
I mean when I see movies like TRANSFORMERS,
like the glut of superhero movies, I go Hmmm?

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:44.580
It’s kind of sad that audiences are responding
to that and making the movie successful, which

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.490
just brings about more movies.&nbsp;

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:52.840
Studios are all about making movies that make
money, so I understand why they keep making

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:53.840
them.&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.170
But for me I’m kind of getting a little
tired of that.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.720
That being said, it doesn’t mean that there
is not good storytelling out there.&nbsp;

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:09.020
I think it’s great that there are more avenues
for stories, Netflix, Amazon, online and all

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.880
this stuff is opening up….The Golden Age
of Television…I think there is much great

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.159
TV being written now than there was before
so all that gives me confidence about the

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:18.310
state of storytelling today.&nbsp;

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.939
I’m kind of happy about that.

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:27.899
I think if you look at the history of entertainment
there has always been both sides, so I’m

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:28.899
kind of in the middle.&nbsp;

00:01:28.899 --> 00:01:32.020
I wouldn’t say “Yes, we’re dumbing down”
or “We’re getting better.”&nbsp;

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.579
It’s both, as it’s has always been.

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:37.859
Film Courage:&nbsp; Do you think we are making
more complex characters these days?

00:01:37.859 --> 00:01:38.859
Karl Iglesias:&nbsp; I think so.&nbsp;

00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:45.810
One of the things about storytelling is that
we’re conditioned to respond to new things,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:46.810
to pay attention to new things.&nbsp;

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:52.109
That is why we like high-concept, part of
it is what I call the uniquely familiar.&nbsp;

00:01:52.109 --> 00:01:55.209
You have to come up with something unique
that we’ve never seen before that is also

00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:56.209
familiar.

00:01:56.209 --> 00:02:02.299
You know this joke about the executive saying
“Yeah, just give me the same thing only

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:03.299
different.”&nbsp;

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:04.299
Right?&nbsp;

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:05.299
You’ve probably heard this before.&nbsp;

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:06.500
That is what it means.&nbsp;

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.869
We want to see stories that are different
but familiar, the same thing but in a different

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:13.780
way, a different angle.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:19.459
The thing with characters is that we see more
and more characters in more and more stories,

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:21.110
we have to come up with something that is
new.&nbsp;

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.060
We have to come up with new characters or
new traits or new goals.&nbsp;

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:31.609
It’s getting harder and harder and harder
so because of that I think they’re becoming

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:32.859
more complex.&nbsp;

00:02:32.859 --> 00:02:37.420
Because of all the entertainment that has
been done, good storytellers are aware of

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:38.420
that.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:46.240
To answer your previous question about beginning
writers, the reason we see a lot of material

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.680
we’ve seen before, kind of like cliché
stuff is because they are not aware of what

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:51.680
has been done before.&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.090
So part of your education as a writer is really
to know what has been done before.&nbsp;

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.099
You really have to be very knowledgable about
this.&nbsp;

00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:01.999
You have to be a geek about this, knowing
all the movies that have been done before,

00:03:01.999 --> 00:03:05.290
knowing all the TV that has been done before,
all the comic books that have been done before

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:09.840
so you know that when you create something
you try to avoid that and come up with something

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:10.840
unique.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.400
There is nothing new under the sun.&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:21.099
Everything has been done before but I am always
amazed and excited when there is a new concept.&nbsp;

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:22.830
I’m like “Oh, my God!&nbsp;

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:23.970
That’s is brilliant.”&nbsp;

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.150
Because it is familiar but it’s unique.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:34.489
It is all about creativity and originality
and that is really a big part of being an

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:35.489
artist.&nbsp;

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:36.489
You have to be original.&nbsp;

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:37.489
That is why we respond to it.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:38.489
Film Courage: [Imitating an executive at a
pitch meeting]&nbsp; “Love it!&nbsp;

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:39.489
But it’s too close to King Lear.&nbsp;

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:40.629
Sorry…”

00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:46.859
Karl Iglesias:&nbsp; [Laughs]&nbsp; Right….but look
what they’ve done with King Lear!&nbsp;

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:50.620
You can modernize it and have a unique angle
on it.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:50.639
Question For Viewers:&nbsp; Are you watching more
television shows than movies?

